3
00:00:15.221 --> 00:00:22.152
<v NARRATOR>Bramble met Mimic on a calm evening, in the middle of a confusing story.

4
00:00:22.152 --> 00:00:47.003
<v NARRATOR>A young creature was telling a tale with three characters — a king, a child, and a dog — but every one sounded exactly the same. "The king said come here and the child said why and the king said because and the dog said woof and the child said no." The listeners got tangled up. Who was talking now?

5
00:00:47.003 --> 00:00:49.254
<v MIMIC>All one voice.

6
00:00:49.254 --> 00:00:54.545
<v MIMIC>And so nobody knows who is speaking.

7
00:00:54.545 --> 00:00:59.436
<v NARRATOR>Bramble jumped. It was as if a second creature had spoken.

8
00:00:59.436 --> 00:01:02.007
<v BRAMBLE>How did you do that?

9
00:01:02.007 --> 00:01:05.418
<v MIMIC>Do what?

10
00:01:05.418 --> 00:01:06.709
<v MIMIC>This?

11
00:01:06.709 --> 00:01:08.440
<v MIMIC>Or this?

12
00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:32.931
<v MIMIC>My name is Mimic. When I tell a story, I give each character their own sound. The king is deep and slow. The child is quick and high. The dog is a low happy grumble. Then the listener always knows who is talking — even if I never say a single name.

13
00:01:32.931 --> 00:01:53.742
<v NARRATOR>To prove it, she told the tangled story again. But this time the king boomed low, the child piped high, and the dog grumbled warm and happy. And suddenly the whole circle could follow every line — they laughed at the dog, they felt the king's grandness.

14
00:01:53.742 --> 00:02:02.433
<v BRAMBLE>My kids' stories get all tangled when there's more than one character. Everyone sounds the same. Would you teach them?

15
00:02:02.433 --> 00:02:22.964
<v MIMIC>I will — and here's the gentle part: you don't have to be a perfect actor. Just give each character ONE small difference. A little higher. A little slower. A little rougher. That one small change is enough for the listener to always know who's speaking.

16
00:02:22.964 --> 00:02:36.135
<v NARRATOR>And Bramble thought how fun it was — to step into another person for just a second — as the circle's kings and children and dogs, at last, each found a voice of their own.

